# One rule decides line endings for everything Git considers text.
#
# eol=lf matters as much as text=auto. On its own, text=auto normalizes to LF inside the repository
# but checks out with the platform default, which is CRLF on Windows. That silently breaks every tracked
# file without an explicit rule: a fresh clone on Windows gets CRLF copies of files whose tools expect
# LF, and the gate's `eol` step then rewrites them one at a time.
#
# Because this covers every path, per-type "text eol=lf" lines are redundant and are not listed. Only
# genuine exceptions appear below.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The one place CRLF is correct: the Windows batch interpreter requires it.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Files Git must not treat as text. "binary" is shorthand for "-text -diff": no line-ending conversion,
# and no attempt to show a textual diff.
#
# The firmware image is the one artifact this repository produces, and it is the one a CI build can
# attach to a release.
*.elf   binary
*.bin   binary
*.map   binary

*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary

# Diff drivers change how a change is displayed, not how it is stored, so these are unaffected by the
# rule above and still carry their weight: Git uses them to label hunks with the enclosing function or
# section.
*.rs       diff=rust
*.toml     diff=toml
*.json     diff=json
*.mjs      diff=javascript
*.yml      diff=yaml
Cargo.lock diff=toml
